common.py:is_bool_dtype`\n- anchor: `pandas/core/dtypes/missing.py:_isna_string_dtype`\n  - module: `pandas.core.dtypes.missing`\n  - definition: `lines 307-324`\n  - related files:\n    - `pandas/core/dtypes/missing.py`: `1` retained path(s)\n  - key dependency paths:\n    - `pandas/core/dtypes/missing.py:_isna_string_dtype`"}, {"content": "Based on the issue description and the supplemental context provided, it seems the key focus is on modifying the plotting functionality of the pandas DataFrame to allow grouping certain columns into the same subplot. The unique artifacts and dependency paths hint at several areas in the pandas codebase that might be relevant for this enhancement.\n\nGiven the context and the nature of the requested feature, we should start by examining the `DataFrame.plot` method and its dependencies in the `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` file. Additionally, other modules such as `pandas/core/indexes/base.py` and `pandas/core/dtypes/missing.py` are mentioned, which could be relevant for handling indexing and data type checks.\n\nLet's first inspect the `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` file, specifically around line 901, which is noted as a unique artifact related to subplots handling.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n<parameter=line_nums>[900, 901, 902, 903, 904, 905]</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 900, 901, 902, 903, 904, 905\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._parse_errorbars`.\nSource: Retrieved code context including pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 900, 901, 902, 903, 904, 905.\n```\n852 | def _parse_errorbars(self, label, err):\n853 |         \"\"\"\n854 |         Look for error keyword arguments and return the actual errorbar data\n855 |         or return the error DataFrame/dict\n856 | \n857 |         Error bars can be specified in several ways:\n858 |             Series: the user provides a pandas.Series object of the same\n859 |                     length as the data\n860 |             ndarray: provides a np.ndarray of the same length as the data\n861 |             DataFrame/dict: error values are paired with keys matching the\n862 |                     key in the plotted DataFrame\n863 |             str: the name of the column within the plotted DataFrame\n864 | \n865 |         Asymmetrical error bars are also supported, however raw error values\n866 |         must be provided in this case. For a ``N`` length :class:`Series`, a\n867 |         ``2xN`` array should be provided indicating lower and upper (or left\n868 |         and right) errors. For a ``MxN`` :class:`DataFrame`, asymmetrical errors\n869 |         should be in a ``Mx2xN`` array.\n870 |         \"\"\"\n871 |         if err is None:\n872 |             return None\n873 | \n874 |         def match_labels(data, e):\n875 |             e = e.reindex(data.index)\n876 |             return e\n877 | \n878 |         # key-matched DataFrame\n879 |         if isinstance(err, ABCDataFrame):\n880 | \n881 |             err = match_labels(self.data, err)\n882 |         # key-matched dict\n883 |         elif isinstance(err, dict):\n884 |             pass\n885 | \n886 |         # Series of error values\n887 |         elif isinstance(err, ABCSeries):\n888 |             # broadcast error series across data\n889 |             err = match_labels(self.data, err)\n890 |             err = np.atleast_2d(err)\n891 |             err = np.tile(err, (self.nseries, 1))\n892 | \n893 |         # errors are a column in the dataframe\n894 |         elif isinstance(err, str):\n895 |             evalues = self.data[err].values\n896 |             self.data = self.data[self.data.columns.drop(err)]\n897 |             err = np.atleast_2d(evalues)\n898 |             err = np.tile(err, (self.nseries, 1))\n899 | \n900 |         elif is_list_like(err):\n901 |             if is_iterator(err):\n902 |                 err = np.atleast_2d(list(err))\n903 |             else:\n904 |                 # raw error values\n905 |                 err = np.atleast_2d(err)\n906 | \n907 |             err_shape = err.shape\n908 | \n909 |             # asymmetrical error bars\n910 |             if isinstance(self.data, ABCSeries) and err_shape[0] == 2:\n911 |                 err = np.expand_dims(err, 0)\n912 |                 err_shape = err.shape\n913 |                 if err_shape[2] != len(self.data):\n914 |                     raise ValueError(\n915 |                         \"Asymmetrical error bars should be provided \"\n916 |                         f\"with the shape (2, {len(self.data)})\"\n917 |                     )\n918 |             elif isinstance(self.data, ABCDataFrame) and err.ndim == 3:\n919 |                 if (\n920 |                     (err_shape[0] != self.nseries)\n921 |                     or (err_shape[1] != 2)\n922 |                     or (err_shape[2] != len(self.data))\n923 |                 ):\n924 |                     raise ValueError(\n925 |                         \"Asymmetrical error bars should be provided \"\n926 |                         f\"with the shape ({self.nseries}, 2, {len(self.data)})\"\n927 |                     )\n928 | \n929 |             # broadcast errors to each data series\n930 |             if len(err) == 1:\n931 |                 err = np.tile(err, (self.nseries, 1))\n932 | \n933 |         elif is_number(err):\n934 |             err = np.tile([err], (self.nseries, len(self.data)))\n935 | \n936 |         else:\n937 |             msg = f\"No valid {label} detected\"\n938 |             raise ValueError(msg)\n939 | \n940 |         return err\n```\n"}, {"content": "The code snippet from `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` around line 901 does not directly relate to subplot grouping. Instead, it pertains to error bar parsing. We need to look at the subplot handling logic.\n\nLet's investigate the `_validate_subplots_kwarg` function in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` as it seems closely related to subplot configuration.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n<parameter=search_terms>[\"_validate_subplots_kwarg\"]</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_validate_subplots_kwarg\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/util/_validators.py:validate_kwargs`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n135 | def validate_kwargs(fname, kwargs, compat_args):\n136 |     \"\"\"\n137 |     Checks whether parameters passed to the **kwargs argument in a\n138 |     function `fname` are valid parameters as specified in `*compat_args`\n139 |     and whether or not they are set to their default values.\n140 | \n141 |     Parameters\n142 |     ----------\n143 |     fname : str\n144 |         The name of the function being passed the `**kwargs` parameter\n145 |     kwargs : dict\n146 |         The `**kwargs` parameter passed into `fname`\n147 |     compat_args: dict\n148 |         A dictionary of keys that `kwargs` is allowed to have and their\n149 |         associated default values\n150 | \n151 |     Raises\n152 |     ------\n153 |     TypeError if `kwargs` contains keys not in `compat_args`\n154 |     ValueError if `kwargs` contains keys in `compat_args` that do not\n155 |     map to the default values specified in `compat_args`\n156 |     \"\"\"\n157 |     kwds = kwargs.copy()\n158 |     _check_for_invalid_keys(fname, kwargs, compat_args)\n159 |     _check_for_default_values(fname, kwds, compat_args)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/util/_validators.